entical utterance. Final answer 100\n---\nLLM extracted final answer: **Step-by-step Analysis:** 1. Let the statement everyone makes be P: “At least one of us is a human.” 2. Two possibilities for its truth value: a) P is true ⇒ There is at least one human. b) P is false ⇒ There are no humans (everyone is a vampire). 3. Behaviour rules: • Humans always tell the truth (can utter only true statements). • Vampires always lie (can utter only false statements). 4. Uniform utterance check: • If P is true, humans could say it, but vampires could not (they would then be speaking truth). Uniformity would break unless everyone were human. • If P is false, then everybody is a vampire; all can uniformly lie by uttering the false sentence. ⇒ Uniform identical replies permit only two homogeneous populations: all-human or all-vampire. 5. Independent evidence: You are “absolutely certain” you saw a resident vampire the night before, so at least one vampire exists. 6. This eliminates the all-human scenario, leaving the all-vampire scenario as the only possibility. 7. The village has 100 residents, so all 100 are vampires. **Final Answer:** \\boxed{100} **Confidence:** 90 **Supporting Evidence:** • Agent summary’s case analysis shows uniform utterance forces either 0 or 100 vampires. • Direct observation guarantees at least one vampire, ruling out 0; thus 100 remains. **Potential Weaknesses:** • Relies on the standard assumption that vampires cannot utter a true statement in its entirety and that “us” refers to all 100 residents. • Assumes no ambiguity in the witness’s certainty about the vampire’s residency. Given the problem’s wording and standard Knights-and-Knaves logic, these assumptions are well supported."
